      "writtenOn": "2021-01-12T23:35:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree that createAudioPatch can return \u0027INVALID_ARGUMENT\u0027 due to other reasons. Clearly, this function offers quite a complex functionality, so testing it isn\u0027t trivial. In fact we don\u0027t currently have VTS tests for \"positive\" cases: https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/+/master:hardware/interfaces/audio/core/all-versions/vts/functional/AudioPrimaryHidlHalTest.h;l\u003d503;drc\u003d3f1457b9537b1ff23704e17be7588dadf1e2884e\n\nSo, I don\u0027t currently know an easy way to know for sure that HAL would not have rejected the proposed arguments, assuming they didn\u0027t contain an invalid enum-string. I\u0027ve added a loop over the valid configs in the test to improve HAL logic coverage. At least, we can state that the HAL is required to return \u0027INVALID_ARGUMENT\u0027 in all of the cases, regardless of the actual cause. Although, I agree that we can\u0027t guarantee that it has actually rejected an invalid enum-string. Added a comment about that.\n\nI disagree that HAL should be using \u0027NOT_SUPPORTED\u0027 error since it\u0027s normally used for the case when this functionality isn\u0027t supported at all. This is stated in the docs: https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/+/master:hardware/interfaces/audio/7.0/types.hal;l\u003d27;drc\u003dec5df2d7473e582e2f726f539b6911d4afa4a5f2. I\u0027ve clarified that in the interface definition.",
